1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,000
This is the job's stage view, and the real defect experiment.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:07,000
The rows are builds.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:15,000
Build number one is green: the baseline, version one point zero point zero, commit six four three five one f e.

4
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:25,000
Build number two, the one with the deliberate defect, is red, and it fails at exactly one place: the unit and integration tests stage.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,000
Build number three is green again.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Build two's console.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:34,000
Pytest reports one failed, eighty one passed.

8
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:41,000
The failure has a name: test overlap rules, with the dates the eighth to the tenth of January 2030.

9
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The assertion says room one oh one was expected to be free, but it was reported booked.

10
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,000
That is exactly the off by one defect.

11
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,000
And here is the quality gate.

12
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Right after the failure, for every following stage Jenkins writes: stage skipped due to earlier failures.

13
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:07,000
E2E tests, package source, build images, publish images, and deploy.

14
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Nothing was packaged, nothing was published, and nothing was deployed.

15
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:18,000
The pipeline ends with its own message: pipeline failed, nothing was deployed.

16
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:25,000
The test result page of build two lists the failing test by name, with its error.

17
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:37,000
This page also contains something surprising, which I will explain in a moment: a package called tests dot e2e with five passed tests, although the Selenium stage never ran in this build.

18
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:40,000
This is the stale report bug.

19
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:47,000
Build two never packaged anything, yet its artifacts list a source archive and a deployment record.

20
00:01:47,000 --> 00:01:48,000
They are not new.

21
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:57,000
They are left over from build number one, because Jenkins reuses its workspace, and the archive step collected the old files.

22
00:01:57,000 --> 00:02:04,000
That is why build two showed five passing Selenium tests: they came from build one's report.

23
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,000
What happened to staging?

24
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:15,000
The deployment record of build one says staging ran version one point zero point zero, commit six four three five one f e.

25
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Because build two's deploy stage was skipped, the staging containers were never touched.

26
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:25,000
So that version stayed active the whole time the defect build was failing.

27
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:31,000
After the corrective commit, build three ran the same pipeline.

28
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:32,000
All stages are green.

29
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:43,000
Its deployment record shows the staging environment now runs version one point zero point zero, commit three zero c six a zero five, the corrected code.

30
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:48,000
So the pipeline blocked the defective version, and released the fixed one.

31
00:02:49,000 --> 00:02:56,000
After I fixed the stale report problem, I repeated the experiment as build number nine.

32
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:58,000
It fails on the same test.

33
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:03,000
But look at its test result: only the tests that really ran.

34
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:06,000
There is no tests dot e2e package any more.

35
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:11,000
The report does not claim Selenium tests that did not happen.

36
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Eighty one passed, one failed, total eighty two.

37
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,000
And the artifacts of build nine: only the one test report that this build produced.

38
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:27,000
No source archive, no deployment record, no health file from earlier builds.

39
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:30,000
The same bug can no longer happen.

40
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:39,000
This is the fix, in every build log, here build eleven: the very first command after checkout is rm minus rf reports dist.

41
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:47,000
Old outputs are deleted before anything runs, so a build can never reuse an old report as if it were new.

42
00:03:47,000 --> 00:03:58,000
That closes the experiment: the defect was caught by the tests, deployment was blocked, the fix was released, and a bug in the pipeline itself was found and fixed.


